{"name":"Haystack","hex":"#cfac47","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Haystack","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A locality in the Kyogle council area, north eastern New South Wales, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Haystack"}],"entries":[{"title":"Haystack (food)","blocks":[{"text":"In the United States, a haystack is a dish composed of a starchy food topped by a protein or rice, in combination with fresh vegetables, and garnished with various condiments. Haystacks are conceptually like a deconstructed tostada. The haystacks ingredients are served individually and assembled on the plate by the person who will be eating it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Majadra_haystack_%28on_the_plate%29_%284291546125%29.jpg/330px-Majadra_haystack_%28on_the_plate%29_%284291546125%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haystack_(food)","wikidata":"Q5687240"},{"title":"Hay","blocks":[{"text":"Hay is grass, legumes, or other herbaceous plants that have been cut and dried to be stored for use as animal fodder, either for large grazing animals raised as livestock, such as cattle, horses, goats, and sheep, or for smaller domesticated animals such as rabbits and guinea pigs. Pigs can eat hay, but they do not digest it as efficiently as herbivores do."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Roundbale1.jpg/330px-Roundbale1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hay","wikidata":"Q336989"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Hayfield2.jpg/500px-Hayfield2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Hayfield2.jpg/500px-Hayfield2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parse